  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1152 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nEarly This Morning through Thursday: Storm Chances Continue\n\nScattered thunderstorms have developed across central to west \ncentral Wisconsin at the time of this writing. The environment \nremains largely supportive for the potential for storms to become \nsevere given sufficient CAPE and shear. The main factor that is \nstill lacking over the area is upper level lift. Storms earlier this \nevening struggled to sustain themselves and the current expectation \nis that the trend will continue, even though the storms right now \nare more widespread and have stronger cores than earlier. It is \nunknown how long these storms may last but they could go for at \nleast a few hours early this morning.\n\nAnother potential for storms, some of which could be severe, may \nreach the far southern counties in our CWA Wednesday morning. A \nshortwave trough, that is currently moving across the Dakotas, will \ncontinue to quickly move east to southeast overnight into Wednesday \nmorning. The forcing provided by that feature combined with \nsufficient moisture will likely lead to yet another storm complex \nmoving towards the Midwest going towards sunrise. Right now, the \ncurrent expectation is that most storms will remain south of \nthe CWA, but a few counties in northeastern Iowa or southwestern\nWisconsin may get clipped by the northern extent of the line. \nModel guidance indicates a tight H700 theta-e gradient setting \nup across northern Iowa towards the WI/IL state line by \nWednesday morning. So the line of storms is anticipated to stay \nalong and south of the gradient. If any storms are able to get \nfar enough north into the area, the potential for severe storms \nwill remain in place with damaging wind gusts being the primary \nhazard. If any storms do move in, they should quickly move east \nout of the area by mid to late morning.\n\nIsolated thunderstorm development will remain possible in the \nafternoon on Wednesday, but the lack of synoptic scale forcing \nshould keep storm coverage on the lower end. Another shower and \nstorm complex will likely move out of the Plains towards the Midwest \non Thursday morning. The potential for severe storms at this time is \nquite low. Even though there is expected to be morning convection \nboth on Wednesday and Thursday, temperatures should be able to rise \ninto the upper 70s to 80s both afternoons.\n\nFriday to Early Next Week: Cooler with Continued Rain Chances\n\nUpper level ridging elongates over the upper Midwest on Friday but \nwill quickly weaken this weekend into early next week as the center \nof high pressure weakens and moves back west towards the southern \nHigh Plains. Even though H850 temperatures increase on Friday into \nSaturday over the region, persistent cloud cover along with the \npotential for rain showers and storms will keep temperatures in the \n70s to low 80s. Speaking of the rain potential, mid level moisture \nincreases Friday into Saturday with southwesterly to westerly flow \nover the region. Shortwaves embedded in the flow will provide \nopportunities for widespread showers and storms, with the highest \nchances (60-80 percent) on Friday night into Saturday morning. Heavy \nrain will be possible with this activity as mean ensemble PWAT \nvalues increase up to 2 inches on Saturday morning. Some flooding \nconcerns may arise given this potential, especially with more \nchances for rain Saturday night into Sunday. Rain chances may \ncontinue into the new work week as shortwaves continue to move over \nthe northern Plains towards the Great Lakes. Temperatures during \nthis time period will remain a few degrees below average in the 70s \nto lower 80s.",
